{
  "file": "src/execution_coverage/progress.rs",
  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[write_line]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Indicatif renders through write_str on this unit-test-only adapter.\n TermLike also requires write_line, whose only behavior delegates to\n that covered recording path. No application input can select this\n unused adapter callback; actual terminal output is tested on a PTY."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[write_line]` is on an explicit function with a body. It is written as fenced commentary, so it counts as a tracked justification, which the built-in requires.\n\nThe reason is substantive, and the source supports each part of it:\n- The `RecordingTerm` adapter is inside `mod[tests]`, so it is unit-test-only.\n- `write_line` only calls `self.write_str(&format!(\"{text}\\n\"))`. `write_str` is the sibling method in the same impl that records the text and frames, and the tests use it through `RecordingTerm`.\n- I did not read indicatif's source. I take the claim that single-line displays render through `write_str` as plausible. Indicatif is known to call `write_line` only for non-final lines of a multi-line draw.\n- The comment says \"No application input can select this unused adapter callback\". This holds because the type is test-only and has no application path.\n- The comment says \"actual terminal output is tested on a PTY\". `tests/authoring_batch.rs` has `Fixture::run_in_pty` and `run_in_pty_with_args`, which use Python's `pty.openpty()`. Several tests call these helpers, including `terminal_progress_distinguishes_judgments_from_authoring_errors` and `empty_batches_and_early_errors_clear_the_terminal_display`.\n\nThe explanation therefore agrees with the current source, and the citation is not redundant or in the wrong role.",
